[E0436] Error: `found` is used after `tree`, which it borrows, was changed
    ╭─[ err/215_what_a_result_borrows/main.wip:46:8 ]
    │
 44 │     val found = tree.find("a")
    │         ──┬──  
    │           ╰──── `found` borrows `tree`
 45 │     tree.nodes.clear()
    │     ─────┬────  
    │          ╰────── `tree` passed as `&var` here
 46 │     if found is .Some(f) {
    │        ──┬──  
    │          ╰──── used here
    │ 
    │ Help: use `found` before `tree` changes, or keep a value that owns its data
    │ 
    │ Note: a view points into what it borrows — a `str` into bytes, a `&` into a place — so that must not change while the view is still used
────╯
[E0436] Error: `text` is used after `short`, which it borrows, was changed
    ╭─[ err/215_what_a_result_borrows/main.wip:56:17 ]
    │
 54 │     val text = short.text()
    │         ──┬─  
    │           ╰─── `text` borrows `short`
 55 │     short.bytes[0] = 'x' as u8
    │     ───────┬──────  
    │            ╰──────── `short` assigned here
 56 │     io::println(text)
    │                 ──┬─  
    │                   ╰─── used here
    │ 
    │ Help: use `text` before `short` changes, or keep a copy that owns its bytes with `String::of(…)`
    │ 
    │ Note: a `str` points into the bytes of what it borrows, so those must not change while the `str` is still used
────╯
[E0437] Error: cannot return an `Option<Found>` that points into `other`
    ╭─[ err/215_what_a_result_borrows/main.wip:66:44 ]
    │
 66 │     fn pick(other: &Tree): Option<Found> = .Some(Found(node: &other.nodes[0]))
    │             ──┬──                          ─────────────────┬─────────────────  
    │               ╰───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── `other` is `&Tree`
    │                                                             │                   
    │                                                             ╰─────────────────── points into `other`
    │ 
    │ Help: answer what `other` borrows, or a value that owns its data
    │ 
    │ Note: `pick`, as `Pick` declares it, answers what its parameters' types can hold, and `other`'s does not hold, as its own, what the result refers to; its callers keep only that
────╯
